WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.427 --> 00:00:08.010
BETH MEAGHER: One
of the things that I

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.880
think was the theme in
everyone's introduction

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.350
and area of focus is this whole
concept of interoperability.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.870
There is a confluence of
events that is happening,

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.900
and it presents us with a
really unique opportunity

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.480
for changing from disease
state to really prediction.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.740
And Andrea, I'd love to hear
a little bit about the data

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.350
strategy that you're
pulling together

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.240
and how that's going
to help further

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.710
the state of the future
of health concept.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.470
ANDREA NORRIS: So NIH,
as we've been going down

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.600
this experiential
journey I call it

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.140
over the last few years
in our data science base,

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.050
and we just issued this past
year our first ever Data

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.340
Science Strategic Plan.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:58.290
Trans NIH, trans
disease, trans domain,

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.330
and I would encourage
you to go on our website

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.980
and take a look at that.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.200
But we identified
five key things.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.570
We really are trying to
look to how we can best

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:10.080
leverage the tremendous
advances in technology

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.770
with our incredible scientific
expertise and research

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:17.170
expertise and harness that
in more powerful ways.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:19.260
And that requires
some cultural change.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.190
Historically, we fund about
300,000 researchers a year

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:27.130
at about 2,500 academic and
medical centers in our country,

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.080
and some cases globally.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.010
And so researchers,
at a time, develop

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.070
their, collect their
data, analyze their data,

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.070
test their hypothesis,
store that data.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.670
We have all of this
data resident anywhere

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.270
you can imagine, right,
someone's desktop,

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.550
someone's server, campus,
medical center, data center,

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:49.600
et cetera.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.310
And we can't find it, we
can't access it easily.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:57.030
We certainly can't interoperate
across various data domains

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.190
and data sets, and
we can't sustain it.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.760
We can't reuse it.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.340
So we're starting over
again, almost every time.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.620
And so our strategic plan really
focuses on five key target

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.430
areas.

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:11.400
First and foremost is to
work more in partnership

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.120
with industry and
non-traditional partners, who

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.300
have the expertise, and
advances, and infrastructure

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:18.940
and compute.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.120
We don't need to buy that
a researcher at a time.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.130
We need to use that
researcher's time

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.590
to do the scientific
advancements,

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:29.130
not to figure out how to set up
my server and my data structure

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:32.460
and what tool I
need to use, right?

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.060
But the other is looking at
the algorithms and the tools.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.830
We fund a great deal
of that, looking

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:42.180
at how we can educate and train
and orient our next generation,

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.380
scientific researchers, with
the tools and capabilities they

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.510
need to take advantage of these
new artificial intelligence,

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:51.790
machine learning,
these vast data sets.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.740
Our big data set programs now
are in the 10s and 20s and 30

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.850
petabytes size ranges.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:02:59.580
These are huge data sets.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.730
We're moving all of these
to the commercial cloud.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.260
We can't sustain
them locally anymore.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:08.010
We want to bring the
researcher to the data

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.580
with relatively available
tools, with their own tools.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.420
They can do compute
by themselves.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.030
They can do it
with someone else.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.480
They can leave what's there
for the next researcher, right?

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.750
And we believe that's
the model of the future.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.740
And so we are trying a lot
of different approaches

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.280
and techniques,
including policies,

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.850
and other things for requiring
data management plans,

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.730
and having people think
about how they're going

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.820
to share and sustain that data.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.330
But we believe that's how
we'll accelerate discovery

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.570
in ways we've never been
able to do in the past.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:46.910
BETH MEAGHER: That's great.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:49.740
Doug, I know that one of the
things that you have been

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.870
thinking about is
working with clients

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.000
to really look at archetypes.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.670
What are the archetypes
in the future of health?

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.260
And I'd love to
hear your thoughts

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.420
on what are some
of the archetypes

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.530
that you think are really--

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.770
you're seeing a lot
more of your clients

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:09.290
talk about, and specifically
maybe some of the challenges?

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:10.440
DOUG BEAUDOIN: Yeah.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.720
We've coined about 10
different archetypes,

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.600
and they range from, as
we talked about here, sort

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.740
of the data convener, the data
analyzer, the data manipulator,

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:23.220
and the interpreter as sort of
one end of the spectrum, all

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:27.240
the way through to how do we
finance this in an equitable

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.080
way, when, if you can predict
the risk of a population's

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.980
disease state or
wellness state, you know,

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.550
what is the role of
health insurance,

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:41.950
both catastrophic
and pooled insurance.

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:44.220
And so that kind of
individual financer

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.220
is a part of the future,
you know, the way

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.540
we buy health insurance could be
dramatically different, because

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.540
of the predictability
of a population

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.910
and predictability of yourself.

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:58.410
I think one of the
other themes that

00:04:58.410 --> 00:04:59.800
has come out in
our point of view

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.592
around the future of
health, and it kind of

00:05:01.592 --> 00:05:04.310
relates to the
concept of getting

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:07.920
the data to the researcher
in your environment,

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.240
we also talk about that
we, as individuals,

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:14.750
will own our own data and
own access to our own data,

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.690
meaning we will be
in the driver's seat,

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:21.380
not only of owning that
data that's either owned now

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:28.250
by a health plan, a research
organization, a health system,

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:30.230
a life sciences corporation.

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:32.480
But how do we create
that interoperability

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.730
so that we can own
it, and we can also

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:37.140
grant access to that data?

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.630
So another kind
of archetype would

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:43.550
be is the idea that the
individual will own the data

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:46.850
and therefore be able to
grant not only access to it,

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:50.250
but they can use their own
data to make some decisions,

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:54.290
especially if you can layer
on a cognitive AI layer

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:57.710
to get them access to the latest
and greatest clinical trials,

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:59.000
as an example.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.940
Don't wait for the
hospitals to suggest it.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:05.450
You know, be doing that kind
of shopping for yourself

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:08.300
for lack of a better
way to put it.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.125
I think the only
other final element

00:06:11.125 --> 00:06:12.500
that I'll talk
about that we talk

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:18.782
about a lot is the retailization
of health care in general.

00:06:18.782 --> 00:06:20.990
I just got off the phone
with the CEO of a big health

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:22.550
system a few minutes ago.

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:25.940
And his point of view is what's
the role of the emergency

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:31.430
room in the future, where
health care is going retail

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:33.710
and that the
consumer is expecting

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:37.460
the same level of retail
experience interaction

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:41.540
that they do at their
very favorite restaurant

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:44.910
and retail situation and
other service providers.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:49.550
So I think the whole
element of the retailization

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:52.680
and whether that becomes like
a health hub kind of concept,

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.760
or whether that becomes
a virtual concept,

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.012
meaning you get care wherever
you want to have care,

00:06:59.012 --> 00:07:00.470
including in your
home, I think are

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:03.350
some of the major shifts
that were predicting.

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:04.580
BETH MEAGHER: That's great.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:06.950
One of the things that
we've been thinking about

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:08.270
are some of the trends.

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:10.640
And Aashima, I know
when we've talked,

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:12.920
you've highlighted
some of the themes

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:17.420
around interoperability and the
connectivity to future health.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:18.710
I'd love to hear from you.

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:21.030
What are the things that
you're excited about?

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:23.780
What are the things that
you're seeing specifically

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:26.840
from your role at Google Cloud?

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:29.720
AASHIMA GUPTA: So what we hear,
much of what we are building

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:31.760
comes from, what we
hear from our partners,

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:34.240
from the industry,
and how do we enable

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:39.410
Andrea and Cardinal Health and
a lot of industry stakeholders.

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:41.180
From the mission
perspective, Google

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:43.290
is a mission-oriented company.

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:45.900
You know us as a
consumer company.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:47.520
And if you're
hearing our keynotes,

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.900
we are making our best
attempt, and we are really

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:54.160
engaging with enterprises
much more deeply.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.230
For past 20 years, we have built
technology from the consumer

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:02.660
world that we are now making
it available to enterprises,

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:06.050
so truly democratization
of infrastructure,

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:09.350
democratization of
artificial intelligence,

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:12.870
and then democratization
of records,

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:14.780
which is the interoperability.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:16.640
So our mission
statement from Google

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:18.500
is a very timeless
statement, which

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:23.660
is to make information
security accessible and useful.

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:26.810
In health care, our mission
is to enable enterprises

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:30.380
to really organize health
care information, make

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:32.340
it accessible, and useful.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:36.470
And if I have to translate
that, when I say organize,

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:38.190
that means security compliance.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:41.480
You need a platform to get the
data that Andrea talked about.

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:43.549
When we say accessible,
that's where

00:08:43.549 --> 00:08:45.500
the interoperability comes in.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:48.860
If the data is locked
up in a system--

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:50.060
and I'll give an example.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:51.990
We are all consumers
of health care,

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:54.260
and this is an
example I use often.

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:55.800
How many health apps--

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:57.200
there's an app for everything.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:59.660
There's a diabetic app.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.110
How many apps do you think
exists in the app stores?

00:09:02.110 --> 00:09:04.160
Any guesses?

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:08.040
Take a guess, like
50,000, 100,000.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.020
It's not a billion yet.

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:14.640
The last I checked it
was like 250,000-plus.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:16.560
Right, there's an
app for everything.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.760
And now I'll ask
a second question.

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:20.440
How many of those
apps do you think

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:24.790
connect to your patient record?

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:25.930
Like 2%.

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:28.750
That's the opportunity, when
we as consumers are given

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:32.140
these tools, and we talk
about patient empowerment,

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:35.200
but if the glass
layer or the app

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:37.480
is not able to connect
to my patient record,

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:41.260
that's where accessibility
and interoperability comes in.

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:44.650
And then the third part is to
make the information useful,

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:47.710
that's where we are applying
AI and machine learning,

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:50.860
and we strongly believe to
apply AI machine learning

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:54.100
in health care where
the data is so diverse,

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:56.610
and the standards
like FHIR, HL7,

00:09:56.610 --> 00:10:00.490
we are teaching a cloud to
speak language of health care.

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:03.500
How many folks here are
familiar with FHIR and HL7?

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:07.490
Well, this is the
health care crowd.

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:08.530
Great.

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:09.970
[INAUDIBLE]

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:11.920
Yeah, so we want to speak--

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:15.790
we are teaching the cloud
from the cloud perspective.

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:18.430
Our engineering team,
our product team,

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:21.130
are focused on building
products solutions

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:26.170
to talk HL7, to talk FHIR
because we believe not only

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:28.570
that data normalization
will be important,

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:31.385
but this is a gateway to
apply AI machine learning,

00:10:31.385 --> 00:10:33.760
because you don't want to
build models, which are working

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:35.560
on just one set of data.

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:39.820
That's where the standardization
and semantic interoperability

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:41.370
with FHIR becomes important.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.560
And as I said before,
we all as a consumer

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:46.130
will benefit from it.

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:52.530
And how many folks have heard
of 21st Century Cures Act?

00:10:52.530 --> 00:11:00.130
Well, so according to
[INAUDIBLE] by 2020,

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:03.320
if patients want
access to their data,

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.580
the provider systems
have responsibility

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:09.430
to give the data in
the form of an API.

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:11.360
It's coming down.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:13.240
This started with
President Obama.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:15.950
So that's where we
will now, as a patient,

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:17.950
that you were talking
about, have access

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.260
to our own data via API.

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:23.040
And the tools that we are
building is to enable that

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:24.502
and teaching those languages.

00:11:24.502 --> 00:11:25.960
BETH MEAGHER: I
think that's great.

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:28.120
And Jon, I know you
have talked about how

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:33.370
Cardinal Health is using AI
and ML to help your business.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:35.530
Would you share with
the audience how you're

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:37.600
thinking about it
now and in the future

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:41.550
and any learnings that you've
seen as you've implemented it?

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:44.570
JON LATSHAW: Yeah, I'd say we're
just at the beginning of it,

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:47.980
so I think a lot of companies
are in the same boat of,

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:49.090
how do we leverage this.

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:54.340
It's not as easy to
consume as we would hope.

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:56.080
So we have data
scientists on staff.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:57.130
They're expensive.

00:11:57.130 --> 00:11:58.840
We need more.

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:03.880
What we're hopeful is, with
Google, AutoML and developments

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:06.790
like that, where me-- and
I'm definitely not a data

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:09.080
scientist-- could
use it and say,

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:11.470
hey, give me some insights
out of this data pool.

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:14.230
I've got a whole set of
data, and I could almost

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.320
ask questions like in a
natural language query

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:17.860
or something like that.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:21.160
I think it's coming, and I think
it'll be faster than we think.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:24.030
But that's a little bit where
we're struggling right now.

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:25.780
We're getting some
benefit, but I

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.570
think it's nothing compared to
what we'll see in five years.

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:30.520
BETH MEAGHER: Any
immediate learnings

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:34.270
that you've seen as you've
started to implement?

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:36.530
JON LATSHAW: I'm not
familiar with that.

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:37.330
BETH MEAGHER: OK.

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:38.920
One of the things
that I think is

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:42.280
interesting with what
each of you have said

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:45.130
is really around ecosystems.

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:48.530
So there are many traditional
folks that you're working with,

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:49.863
and you'll continue.

00:12:49.863 --> 00:12:51.280
But one of the
themes that I think

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:54.970
has come out in each of
your sort of future talks

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:59.680
about where the health market's
going is working with people

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:01.160
that are slightly different.

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:02.680
So Andrea, I'd love to hear--

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:06.760
I know you all at NIH are doing
some really interesting things

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:09.167
around sickle cell and
around rare diseases.

00:13:09.167 --> 00:13:10.750
And I'd like to hear
a little bit more

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:13.200
about how these ecosystems
are playing out.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:17.050
ANDREA NORRIS: Yeah,
so we've tended

00:13:17.050 --> 00:13:22.420
to study by disease or by
data domain or data type.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:23.620
And I'll share with you.

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:28.000
So 2003, we did the
Human Genome program.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:35.590
That was $3 billion and took
decades, more than 10 years

00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:37.460
to be able to
solve that problem.

00:13:40.435 --> 00:13:41.290
That was 2003.

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:46.630
2010, just seven years later,
it was $10 million and months.

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:52.490
Now it's less than $1,000
and minutes to do this.

00:13:52.490 --> 00:13:54.820
So in that same
window of time, we

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:57.270
saw the rise of the
electronic health record

00:13:57.270 --> 00:14:01.060
and went from zero adoption
to almost full adoption.

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:02.680
But we haven't
figured out really

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:04.590
how to take advantage of that.

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:07.720
And incredible changes
in imaging data--

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:10.420
so MRI and scanning data.

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:14.470
So what we're seeing
is incredible advances

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:19.960
in discovery, in diseases that
we just could never deal with.

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:22.540
One of our programs, we have a
Precision Medicine Initiative.

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:24.940
We believe this is
a key way that we're

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:26.650
going to accelerate discovery.

00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:29.080
We're looking for
a million cohorts.

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:30.520
And it's participants.

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:32.555
We literally changed
the language.

00:14:32.555 --> 00:14:33.430
You're not a patient.

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:36.910
You're a participant in
the research journey.

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:40.810
You contribute your genome data,
your electronic health record,

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:43.930
your environmental data, so
we know your Fitbit data,

00:14:43.930 --> 00:14:47.210
so we know about
you in your world,

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:51.340
in your history, how you
live, and about your health,

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:55.450
and along with a million other
cohorts, a very diverse people.

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:57.130
Now, why does that matter?

00:14:57.130 --> 00:15:00.430
Think what you can do with
preliminary clinical trial

00:15:00.430 --> 00:15:05.170
ideas if you have access
to that kind of rich data

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:10.150
across diverse over multiple
years that can accelerate

00:15:10.150 --> 00:15:12.490
the billion dollars
and 10 years it

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:17.140
takes to get a healthy drug,
a successful drug to market.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:20.170
There's like a 95% failure rate.

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:24.280
So if you can do
virtual clinical trials

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:27.610
in these rich data
sets, think how much

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:29.620
we can learn from that.

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:32.980
Alzheimer's-- every 66
seconds someone is diagnosed

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:35.055
in our country with Alzheimer's.

00:15:35.055 --> 00:15:36.430
Just the time
we've been up here,

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:38.650
think how many people that is.

00:15:38.650 --> 00:15:43.480
This is a crippling economic
and devastating disease

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:45.140
for our country.

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:49.990
And were looking not only in
how we can solve the problem,

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:51.820
treat the disease,
but also how we

00:15:51.820 --> 00:15:54.670
can use computational
capabilities to predict well

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:57.880
in advance symptoms and
signs of people based

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:00.520
on their keyboarding skills,
based on their movement

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:06.310
and action, in home, in situ,
to help get you to the drugs

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:07.900
and treatments sooner.

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:11.480
We think sickle cell
is on the horizon.

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:14.620
We're going to have one of the
first HIV-free generations.

00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:18.020
I believe, in my
lifetime, I will see that.

00:16:18.020 --> 00:16:25.000
And so these diseases,
we can deal with them.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:26.980
We may not be able
to resolve them all,

00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:28.210
but we can treat them.

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:30.940
We can get treatments
to the market faster.

00:16:30.940 --> 00:16:36.040
And it's this data, this merger
and intersection of the ability

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:41.230
to access data, to
have more participant,

00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:47.210
person-centric health
in your health journey,

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:51.340
and the computational
capabilities, that is just--

00:16:51.340 --> 00:16:53.590
it is game-changing.

00:16:53.590 --> 00:16:56.470
Today a computer is as much
a scientific instrument

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:59.810
as a microscope
was 15 years ago.

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:04.162
And so it's an exciting future.

00:17:04.162 --> 00:17:06.312
AASHIMA GUPTA: I think
I would add to that,

00:17:06.312 --> 00:17:08.770
I think when you are talking
about artificial intelligence,

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:13.690
machine learning, maybe
I have a little more view

00:17:13.690 --> 00:17:16.930
from the pragmatic
approach in health care,

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:20.420
even the connectivity
itself is a big issue.

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:23.950
AI is a next step,
but I submit to you,

00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:25.839
if you don't have a
solid data strategy,

00:17:25.839 --> 00:17:29.110
building an AI strategy, it's
not going to take you far.

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:31.870
So I'm good with
examples-- one more

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:33.860
example I'll share with you.

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:37.030
So let's say today, we all
talk about digital campaign,

00:17:37.030 --> 00:17:39.400
reaching out to our consumer,
really helping them,

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:42.080
empowering them to take
care of their health.

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:44.770
So today, let's say if we
are running a query, maybe

00:17:44.770 --> 00:17:49.870
on population health, where you
want to make an outreach to all

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:54.990
the females who are age from
45 to 50 who have a BRCA gene,

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:58.810
BRCA1 and BRCA2, had the
mammogram three years ago,

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:03.400
haven't checked into their
physician in the past 18

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:04.210
months.

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:05.740
There's no predictability here.

00:18:05.740 --> 00:18:07.810
You're looking
backwards, and you

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:10.660
want to find out and make an
outreach to that population,

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:14.530
because they need to be come and
see and get the mammogram done.

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:16.750
How many folks here
in this room think

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:20.860
we can connect all these things
today and run such a query?

00:18:20.860 --> 00:18:22.210
There's no AI here.

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:23.950
One person, OK?

00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:25.170
Well, you sir are ahead.

00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:30.790
Because why?

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:34.220
Because your imaging are living
in some PACS system in data

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:34.720
center.

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:36.850
Your health records are in HL7.

00:18:36.850 --> 00:18:39.610
Your paying information,
what time it was due

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:41.830
is in your membership system.

00:18:41.830 --> 00:18:43.130
How do you connect that?

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:46.600
And that's where the
cross-modality analytics

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:50.260
requires a platform
like cloud where you're

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:52.930
bringing in this data,
you're normalizing it,

00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:57.040
and you're able to run those
queries to make that outreach

00:18:57.040 --> 00:18:59.080
to your patient,
to your consumers,

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:02.990
to really help them with
their journey to be healthy.

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:07.660
And once you have a solid data
strategy, the data analytics,

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:10.720
you can really
build an AI layer.

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:14.560
What I worry now when I do
talk to a lot of customers,

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:15.310
oh, we want AI.

00:19:15.310 --> 00:19:18.160
It's not a pixie dust.

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:21.250
Yes, we want AI, but you
really need a solid data.

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:22.210
And it is hard work.

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:23.200
It's mundane.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:25.540
It's complicated.

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:29.840
And that's where we want
to help in taking and--

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:33.160
I call, I say
glorified plumbers,

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:36.280
to get those pipes to get
this data normalize it.

00:19:36.280 --> 00:19:38.140
This is what Google is good at.

00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:40.180
And make it easy
so that then you

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:44.190
can build your next level of
artificial intelligence tools

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:47.470
and do the good things
and innovate and build

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:49.862
those really empowering
capabilities.

00:19:49.862 --> 00:19:51.320
BETH MEAGHER: I
think that's great.

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:53.530
And Doug, I'd love to
hear a little bit from you

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:57.640
around, when we think about
the archetypes, what archetype

00:19:57.640 --> 00:20:02.480
do you see Deloitte playing
now and in the future?

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:05.450
DOUG BEAUDOIN: I would
say it's, in my opinion,

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:08.560
from a Deloitte perspective,
we have a unique situation

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:10.420
where we get the
opportunity to work

00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:14.350
with many, many different parts
of the ecosystem that don't

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:16.810
normally play well together.

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:20.380
If you think about
retailers and health care,

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:22.960
it's not natural for
them to play together,

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:23.950
but we're seeing that.

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:26.710
The retailization of
health care is happening.

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:30.820
And we're in a unique way,
because we have the opportunity

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:33.040
to serve all the health
care organizations

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:36.940
and the government, plus
big retailers as well.

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:40.720
So driving that ecosystem,
play that integration.

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:44.410
Obviously many of those
retailers are making

00:20:44.410 --> 00:20:46.690
plays themselves--

00:20:46.690 --> 00:20:49.730
Aetna and CVS coming together.

00:20:49.730 --> 00:20:52.630
I think we're going to
see the kind of burgeoning

00:20:52.630 --> 00:20:56.800
retailization of health care
and those big retailers getting

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:57.580
into health care.

00:20:57.580 --> 00:21:00.310
And in my opinion, bring it on.

00:21:00.310 --> 00:21:02.660
I think we need to innovate.

00:21:02.660 --> 00:21:06.580
I also think we have
this unique opportunity

00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:10.840
to work with all of the really
important technology giants.

00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:13.840
And again, we're here
talking about Google,

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:16.360
but in my opinion, bring it on.

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:20.830
The ability and the innovation
that the tech giants can bring

00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:23.220
I think is a really,
really important element

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:27.550
to innovate health care way
faster than we've done before.

00:21:27.550 --> 00:21:31.000
The third element that I think
is kind of a forgotten a little

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:33.730
bit element of our
health care system,

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:36.295
and that is the associations--
the Canadian Cancer--

00:21:36.295 --> 00:21:38.260
or the American
Cancer Association--

00:21:38.260 --> 00:21:40.090
you can tell I'm from Canada--

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:42.860
the MS Society.

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:45.790
And I think we're going to see
a new lease on life in the trust

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:47.890
factor that those
organizations have

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:52.030
had in a disease-specific
way and what role they

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:57.550
can play around data
sharing, providing insights.

00:21:57.550 --> 00:21:59.710
There's some great
examples out there

00:21:59.710 --> 00:22:02.590
where, for different
disease states,

00:22:02.590 --> 00:22:07.240
the association is getting
together with Google,

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:10.670
as an example, and
creating that trust.

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:14.740
Again, the movement towards you
owning your data is happening,

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:15.760
we talked about.

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:20.560
But the movement towards you
sharing your data into a pool

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:23.650
to allow for greater
advancement and discovery

00:22:23.650 --> 00:22:25.360
is a little bit
slower on the uptake.

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:28.330
And I think that the
associations play a really key

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:33.250
role, and we're helping kind
of stitch those partnerships

00:22:33.250 --> 00:22:33.790
together.

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:35.500
BETH MEAGHER: That's great.

00:22:35.500 --> 00:22:38.600
Interestingly, as you're all
talking about the future,

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:40.270
one thing is clear--

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:42.490
things are going
to be different.

00:22:42.490 --> 00:22:46.270
And I'm curious, Jon, as you
start to think about the team

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:50.350
that you need to really move
forward, how does it look?

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:51.520
Does it look any different?

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:53.103
Are there different
skills that you're

00:22:53.103 --> 00:22:55.570
going to need from the
workforce to help really

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:58.018
support your organization?

00:22:58.018 --> 00:23:00.310
JON LATSHAW: Yeah, that's
going to change dramatically.

00:23:00.310 --> 00:23:03.840
First, I want the pixie dust.

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:05.755
You guys should trademark
that right now--

00:23:05.755 --> 00:23:08.620
just Pixie Dust AI.

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:09.530
Yeah, definitely.

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:13.360
I think everybody-- and
we're seeing this just

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:15.885
across all industries.

00:23:15.885 --> 00:23:17.260
Everybody's becoming
a little bit

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:20.830
of a computer scientist, a
little bit of a data scientist.

00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:23.050
It used to be,
there was the nerds,

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:24.730
and there was everybody else.

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:27.100
And the nerds would sit, and
they would stitch together

00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:29.230
the computers, and
they'd write code.

00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:32.920
But now everybody,
just every profession,

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:35.800
you have to learn at least
a little bit about computers

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:39.650
and data analytics.

00:23:39.650 --> 00:23:42.798
We're dealing with this
at Cardinal Health in IT

00:23:42.798 --> 00:23:43.840
as we shift to the cloud.

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:46.220
Everybody's like,
what should we learn?

00:23:46.220 --> 00:23:49.020
And it's like, well, I
can't tell you exactly what,

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:51.580
but here's some good ideas,
things that won't ever

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:52.900
go out of style.

00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:54.970
And I think that's
going to apply

00:23:54.970 --> 00:23:58.468
to even professions-- lawyers,
doctors, medical professionals.

00:23:58.468 --> 00:24:00.760
Everybody is going to have
to be a little more computer

00:24:00.760 --> 00:24:01.705
literate.

00:24:01.705 --> 00:24:02.830
BETH MEAGHER: That's great.

00:24:02.830 --> 00:24:08.200
Andrea, you are the CIO of
one of the coolest missions

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:09.790
in the United States.

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:13.390
And we're here with
some great tech leaders.

00:24:13.390 --> 00:24:15.310
What are some of
the things that you

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:19.000
would like to see from people
that are interested in working

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:21.190
at NIH, and why come to NIH?

00:24:21.190 --> 00:24:24.170
ANDREA NORRIS: Yeah,
partnership, first of all--

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:31.240
we're looking for partners,
not providers, not here's

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:37.150
the answer to your problem
if you just buy this.

00:24:37.150 --> 00:24:40.870
We really are in an
experiential journey.

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:42.370
That's what we do.

00:24:42.370 --> 00:24:45.550
Research is a long-term
horizon, but we

00:24:45.550 --> 00:24:50.370
want to make it more agile,
more facile, more adaptable,

00:24:50.370 --> 00:24:52.600
and more expedient.

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:57.160
So we need people who are
committed to that mission, who

00:24:57.160 --> 00:25:01.290
want to advance discovery and
accelerate discovery and help

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:04.520
for people all around the world.

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:09.600
And so I like to say,
NIH has a big footprint.

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:12.740
So whatever you do,
it might seem small,

00:25:12.740 --> 00:25:15.280
but it has impacts
on people's lives

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:18.280
truly around the world
in ways that you'll

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:20.920
never, ever fully realize.

00:25:20.920 --> 00:25:23.530
There's never a time that I
don't talk to groups like this

00:25:23.530 --> 00:25:25.780
that someone doesn't
come up and say,

00:25:25.780 --> 00:25:28.960
my mother was at the clinical
center, the world's largest

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:31.090
clinical research
hospital in the world,

00:25:31.090 --> 00:25:34.030
and it was that research
that saved her life.

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:40.330
Or my son is in medical school
and was funded and supported

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:42.820
by the National
Institutes of Health

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:46.780
and trained under those dollars.

00:25:46.780 --> 00:25:48.740
Health is personal, right?

00:25:48.740 --> 00:25:52.510
It's your health, your family's
health, your children's health.

00:25:52.510 --> 00:25:55.330
There's nothing
more precious to us

00:25:55.330 --> 00:25:58.540
than our health and well-being.

00:25:58.540 --> 00:26:01.280
So it is such an
honor to be at NIH,

00:26:01.280 --> 00:26:04.600
and it is such an
exciting time to be there.

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:07.840
We are looking--
traditionally, we hired--

00:26:07.840 --> 00:26:10.870
they called them the
wet lab researchers--

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:14.380
bioinformatics people,
biologists, chemistry.

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:18.280
What we know about the
biological system in health

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:20.980
is so small compared
to what we need

00:26:20.980 --> 00:26:25.550
to know, after all of these
years and all of this time.

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:29.720
And so now we need data
scientists, we need engineers,

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:34.540
we need technologists,
we need communicators,

00:26:34.540 --> 00:26:38.320
we need that kind
of team science

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:42.830
to really help integrate and
merge some of these ideas.

00:26:42.830 --> 00:26:44.590
That's where the
discovery's going to be.

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:48.070
It's not going to be in
the traditional vertical.

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:50.320
There will still be
discoveries there,

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:53.170
but the ones that
will be game-changing

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:55.600
are going to come from
this cross-fertilization

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:59.860
and diversity of experience,
background, and perspectives.

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:02.940
We believe that very strongly.

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:06.240
BETH MEAGHER: Aashima, maybe one
question before we open it up

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:07.710
to the audience.

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:09.150
We've talked a lot about data.

00:27:09.150 --> 00:27:10.950
We've talked about
interoperability.

00:27:10.950 --> 00:27:14.580
I'd like to hear your
views on security

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:16.890
and the things that are
sort of top of mind for you

00:27:16.890 --> 00:27:18.125
in that regard.

00:27:18.125 --> 00:27:19.750
AASHIMA GUPTA: That's
a great question.

00:27:19.750 --> 00:27:25.230
And before I answer that, having
come from the Kaiser world

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:28.980
and having built those systems--

00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:31.440
I know legacy is a bad word,
but I'm still going to use it.

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:34.620
These systems that have been
built in the health care

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:37.110
assume the patient to
be in the facility.

00:27:37.110 --> 00:27:39.960
If I got sick, I went
and saw the doctor,

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:42.480
be [? TR, ?] be my
primary care physician.

00:27:42.480 --> 00:27:45.330
That paradigm has
changed on its head.

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:48.720
Now people are expecting
care where they are,

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:52.410
but the systems are not designed
to be accessed from outside.

00:27:52.410 --> 00:27:53.880
When you talk
about ecosystem, we

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:57.090
are talking about telehealth,
we're talking about e-visits.

00:27:57.090 --> 00:27:59.220
All the systems that
have been in place,

00:27:59.220 --> 00:28:02.520
they served a purpose when
the people were visiting

00:28:02.520 --> 00:28:04.860
physically in that facility.

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:08.160
And now when we're moving to
care-where-you-are paradigm,

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:11.100
that security layer
becomes very important.

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:13.560
People don't know how
to open the systems.

00:28:13.560 --> 00:28:15.150
How do you do single sign-on?

00:28:15.150 --> 00:28:16.240
What is an OAuth?

00:28:16.240 --> 00:28:19.910
These are basic
skills, and we want

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:21.410
to meet the industry
where they are.

00:28:21.410 --> 00:28:24.120
And building a lot of
security-based portfolio,

00:28:24.120 --> 00:28:26.550
it's super important to us.

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:29.160
Google Cloud is HIPAA
compliant, but we are all

00:28:29.160 --> 00:28:31.560
building new in
brand capabilities,

00:28:31.560 --> 00:28:36.360
and HIPAA security is the
paradigm there-- like security,

00:28:36.360 --> 00:28:38.460
auditability, who has
access to your system

00:28:38.460 --> 00:28:40.680
when you're talking
about partners.

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:42.490
So that becomes one part.

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:47.970
The second part I would say
is focusing on the innovation

00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:51.840
and letting the
infrastructure be--

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:53.190
I would say for Google Cloud.

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:57.570
But that's where we want to
align our [? course ?] trends

00:28:57.570 --> 00:28:59.980
and provide value
to the industry.

00:28:59.980 --> 00:29:02.550
So if you are passionate
about racking and stacking

00:29:02.550 --> 00:29:06.690
and designing the tips of the
infrastructure, join Google.

00:29:06.690 --> 00:29:09.240
But I would want you
to really work on,

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:11.700
how do you build those
capabilities on this world

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:13.200
class infrastructure,
because that's

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:17.140
where the democratization
will come from.

00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:21.810
So don't solve to solve
problems, is my message to you.

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:24.750
Solve the problem that our
patients need, like we need,

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:26.250
like consumers need.

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:28.200
And the problems
that have been solved

00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:31.590
by the big tech or
the infrastructure,

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:33.390
we take security very seriously.

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:35.460
We have hundreds of
security engineers,

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:38.400
and they're experts
in their field, that

00:29:38.400 --> 00:29:40.470
are available to you now.

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:43.590
And that's where the true
democratization comes in.

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:45.870
And the third thing
I would say is,

00:29:45.870 --> 00:29:48.960
cloud is not just a
lift and shift strategy,

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:51.140
and I do see that even today.

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:52.560
It's truly transformation.

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:55.440
It's a means to an end.

00:29:55.440 --> 00:29:58.103
If you've just taken a
server from your on-premise

00:29:58.103 --> 00:29:59.520
and moved to the
cloud, that's not

00:29:59.520 --> 00:30:01.260
what this
transformation is about.

00:30:01.260 --> 00:30:04.680
It's truly looking into the
business transformation,

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:07.440
looking into, like in this
care-where-you-are paradigm,

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:11.670
getting that out there, and
cloud be that means to the end.

00:30:11.670 --> 00:30:13.740
And you define the
end, your strategy.

00:30:13.740 --> 00:30:15.202
That's where we need to get to.

00:30:15.202 --> 00:30:16.660
BETH MEAGHER: I
think that's great.

00:30:16.660 --> 00:30:19.050
And Andrea, I know this is
really important to you.

00:30:19.050 --> 00:30:20.818
Anything to add on
the security front?

00:30:20.818 --> 00:30:23.110
ANDREA NORRIS: No, I just
think, in the research world,

00:30:23.110 --> 00:30:26.490
one of the things that's really
important for us is that we

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:30.600
protect the data, not just for
security and privacy reasons,

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:32.970
but to honor the
consent agreement

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:39.210
by which you consent to give
your very important data to be

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:41.430
used for research purposes.

00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:46.440
And we build those controls
then to honor that consent.

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:48.780
It may be that I
only want it used

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:52.590
if it's federal funded and
not for a profit entity,

00:30:52.590 --> 00:30:55.810
or if it's in particular
areas of study.

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:57.903
And so that's a
level of complexity

00:30:57.903 --> 00:30:59.820
that we've been dealing
with, and particularly

00:30:59.820 --> 00:31:04.800
as these data sets have
gotten so big, and one

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:06.990
that we continue to work on.

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:10.380
Our studies and
surveys and work has

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:12.900
found that people are
very willing to contribute

00:31:12.900 --> 00:31:14.970
their data for
the bigger cause--

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:19.140
my health, the betterment
of people around the world.

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:23.400
But they want the assurance
that my privacy, what

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:28.380
I'm consenting this data
for, is going to be protected

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:30.690
and that there
won't be any issues

00:31:30.690 --> 00:31:35.010
with it being used for purposes
other than what I give it.

00:31:35.010 --> 00:31:38.010
And that's something we spend
a lot of time and effort

00:31:38.010 --> 00:31:41.520
and money trying to
make sure we honor that.

00:31:41.520 --> 00:31:44.130
We're absolutely
committed to that,

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:48.120
because that's what is
reasonable and should

00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:49.872
be what people should expect.

00:31:49.872 --> 00:31:51.330
And I think as we
move more towards

00:31:51.330 --> 00:31:55.890
this participant-managed, you're
managing your health care,

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:58.950
your health profile,
that's going to continue

00:31:58.950 --> 00:32:01.500
to be even more important.

00:32:01.500 --> 00:32:04.700
BETH MEAGHER: So I would
love to hear one word that

00:32:04.700 --> 00:32:07.590
makes you kind of summarize
the future of health,

00:32:07.590 --> 00:32:10.650
that describes what you're
thinking as the future comes,

00:32:10.650 --> 00:32:11.150
Andrea.

00:32:11.150 --> 00:32:12.275
DOUG BEAUDOIN: Here you go.

00:32:12.275 --> 00:32:14.570
Sorry.

00:32:14.570 --> 00:32:15.680
ANDREA NORRIS: Optimistic.

00:32:18.237 --> 00:32:19.320
AASHIMA GUPTA: Empowering.

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:24.920
JON LATSHAW: I was going
to say optimistic, but--

00:32:24.920 --> 00:32:26.270
BETH MEAGHER: And empowering.

00:32:26.270 --> 00:32:28.132
JON LATSHAW: Hopeful.

00:32:28.132 --> 00:32:29.840
DOUG BEAUDOIN: I'm
going to say wellness.

00:32:29.840 --> 00:32:30.798
BETH MEAGHER: Wellness.

00:32:30.798 --> 00:32:31.660
So thank you.

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:33.170
Incredible dialogue, thank you.

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:33.770
Great job.

00:32:33.770 --> 00:32:34.270
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:34.270 --> 00:32:37.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

